G53.6 under the machine frame kinematics (G13.1) on the table machine of
twp-static, whose INI and HAL files and helpers it shares;
twp-static-tip-head runs it on the head machine.

G53.6 under G13.1 keeps the tip on the part the whole way, not only at the
ends: status part_position stays on its point while the rotaries turn, and
the move ends where the TCP kinematics ends it, G13.1 still in force; a
second G53.6 there has nothing to turn and is no move.  An
abort and a feed hold part way leave the tip on its point, and the machine
goes on from there to the same end.  A slide that swings past its limit
half way round, the tool turning from one side of the vertical to the
other, refuses G53.6 under G13.1 as under the TCP kinematics.

These moves run the rotaries at 180 deg/s, set through the INI's HAL pins:
the joints between servo cycles come from motion's interpolation, which
puts the tip off the path by an amount that grows with the square of the
speed.
